## 3. What a Server Is & What a Cloud Provider Rents You

A **server** is just a computer whose job is to stay running and respond to requests from other computers (clients) — the same kind of 
hardware as any PC, just dedicated to listening and answering rather than being used directly by one person.

A **cloud provider** (AWS, Azure, GCP) owns thousands of these physical servers in data centers, and what it actually rents you is a 
virtual *slice* of that hardware — created through virtualization software that splits one physical machine into many independent virtual 
ones — giving you compute (CPU/RAM), storage, and networking on demand, billed by usage instead of requiring you to buy, rack, and 
maintain the physical machine yourself.

| You rent... | What it actually is |
|---|---|
| **Compute** | A slice of CPU + RAM on a physical server — your own virtual machine (e.g. AWS EC2 instance) |
| **Storage** | Disk space on their storage systems (e.g. AWS S3, EBS) |
| **Networking** | Bandwidth, IP addresses, load balancers, routing between your resources and the internet |
| **Managed services** | Pre-configured software you don't have to install/patch yourself (e.g. managed databases, Kubernetes clusters) |
